00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:42.030
Super powerful.

00:22:42.030 --> 00:22:44.880
And let's do a quick
tour of the architecture

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:46.560
that powers the
Wayfinder web app.

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:50.070
So, starting at the top here you
can see the front end is written

00:22:50.070 --> 00:22:51.030
in React.

00:22:51.030 --> 00:22:55.200
So, a user submits a query like
"Plan my trip to Yosemite."

00:22:55.200 --> 00:22:58.440
That sends an HTP request to
the backend which is written

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:01.770
in Python running
Fast API Server.

00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:04.410
That Python backend
does an API query

00:23:04.410 --> 00:23:08.220
to the Elastic stack running
the Trip Planner agent

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:09.810
that we just created.

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:14.250
And you can see that the Agent
Builder agent has access

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:15.720
to the four tools
that we gave it.

00:23:15.720 --> 00:23:17.100
And starting over on this side,

00:23:17.100 --> 00:23:20.280
you can see the Agent
Builder has a tool called

00:23:20.280 --> 00:23:22.470
"Product Search" that
does semantic search

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:24.300
against the product catalog.

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:26.670
It also has a tool called
"Get User Affinity"

00:23:26.670 --> 00:23:32.040
which does an ESQL query
to the user's clickstream.

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:35.790
Additionally, it has a tool
called "Check Trip Safety"

00:23:35.790 --> 00:23:38.970
and that goes through a
workflow that does an HTP post

00:23:38.970 --> 00:23:41.640
to an MCP server to get
weather information.

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:45.240
And finally, the tool that we
created, "Get Customer Profile,"

00:23:45.240 --> 00:23:48.000
goes through a workflow
called "Get Customer Profile"

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:52.410
that does an HTP post to
an external MCP server

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:55.500
that queries a CRM service.

00:23:55.500 --> 00:24:00.270
And all of that creates the
trip planner that you just saw.

